"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[948],{800948:e=>{e.exports="# Data Types\n\nSnowflake scalar type → DBSQL type mapping. Includes precision drift and behavioral differences for non-semi-structured types.\n\n> Semi-structured types (`VARIANT`, `OBJECT`, `ARRAY`) and their construction / conversion functions live in `references/snowflake/03-semi-structured.md`.\n\n## SNOWFLAKE\n\n### Types with behavioral differences\n\n| Snowflake                                                                 | DBSQL                                                       | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                      |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `NUMBER` / `NUMBER(38,0)`                                                 | `DECIMAL(38,0)`                                             | Snowflake `NUMBER` default is `(38,0)`. DBSQL `DECIMAL` max precision is also 38.                                                                                                                          |\n| `NUMBER(p,s)` / `DECIMAL(p,s)` / `NUMERIC(p,s)`                           | `DECIMAL(p,s)` / `NUMERIC(p,s)`                             | Direct map                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| `INT` / `INTEGER` / `BIGINT` / `SMALLINT` / `TINYINT` / `BYTEINT`         | **All aliases for `NUMBER(38,0)` in Snowflake**             | **Silent overflow risk** — see _Integer precision drift_ below                                                                                                                                             |\n| `FLOAT` / `FLOAT4` / `FLOAT8` / `DOUBLE` / `DOUBLE PRECISION` / `REAL`    | `DOUBLE`                                                    | Snowflake stores all float variants as 64-bit IEEE double. DBSQL `FLOAT` is 4-byte; use `DOUBLE` to preserve precision.                                                                                    |\n| `VARCHAR` / `VARCHAR(N)` / `STRING` / `TEXT` / `CHAR(N)` / `CHARACTER(N)` | `STRING`                                                    | All Snowflake string types are Unicode VARCHAR up to 128 MB. DBSQL `STRING` is unbounded UTF-8.                                                                                                            |\n| `VARCHAR(N)` with length enforcement                                      | `VARCHAR(N)`                                                | DBSQL `VARCHAR(N)` is a length-constrained variant of STRING — preserve when the source relies on length limits                                                                                            |\n| `CHAR(N)`                                                                 | `STRING` (preferred) or `VARCHAR(N)` for length enforcement | **Snowflake does not pad `CHAR` values** (despite the type name). DBSQL `CHAR(N)` may pad with spaces on read (controlled by `spark.sql.readSideCharPadding`) — prefer `STRING` to avoid surprise padding. |\n| `BINARY` / `VARBINARY`                                                    | `BINARY`                                                    | Same                                                                                                                                                                                                       |\n| `BOOLEAN`                                                                 | `BOOLEAN`                                                   | Same                                                                                                                                                                                                       |\n| `DATE`                                                                    | `DATE`                                                      | Same                                                                                                                                                                                                       |\n| `TIMESTAMP_NTZ` (no time zone)                                            | `TIMESTAMP_NTZ`                                             | DBSQL has native `TIMESTAMP_NTZ` (DBR 13.3 LTS+). On older runtimes map to `TIMESTAMP` and flag the TZ semantics change.                                                                                   |\n| `TIMESTAMP_LTZ` (stored as UTC, displayed in session TZ)                  | `TIMESTAMP`                                                 | DBSQL `TIMESTAMP` behaves like `TIMESTAMP_LTZ` — UTC-normalized, session-TZ display                                                                                                                        |\n| `TIMESTAMP_TZ` (stores per-value offset)                                  | `TIMESTAMP`                                                 | **Per-value offset is lost** when stored as `TIMESTAMP`. Normalize to UTC before export, or preserve the offset in a separate `STRING` column.                                                             |\n| `TIMESTAMP` (alias; resolves to `TIMESTAMP_NTZ` / `LTZ` based on session) | `TIMESTAMP_NTZ` (safe default) or `TIMESTAMP`               | The session parameter `TIMESTAMP_TYPE_MAPPING` controls which flavor `TIMESTAMP` resolves to. Check source before choosing the DBSQL target.                                                               |\n| `TIME`                                                                    | `STRING` (`HH:mm:ss` format)                                | DBSQL has no native `TIME` type. Store as string; use `CAST(x AS TIMESTAMP)` for arithmetic or `DATE_FORMAT` for display.                                                                                  |\n| `GEOGRAPHY`                                                               | `GEOGRAPHY(4326)`                                           | Native type DBR 17.1+ (preview). WGS84 only — matches Snowflake `GEOGRAPHY`. Not supported on Iceberg tables. Fallback: `STRING` in WKT with `ST_GEOGFROMTEXT()`.                                          |\n| `GEOMETRY`                                                                | `GEOMETRY(srid)`                                            | Native type DBR 17.1+ (preview). Planar math, consistent with Snowflake. Snowflake default SRID is 0 (unspecified); DBSQL preserves SRID.                                                                  |\n\n### Identical types (no translation)\n\n`BOOLEAN`, `DATE`, `DECIMAL(p,s)`, `NUMERIC(p,s)`, `BINARY`.\n\n### Integer precision drift (CRITICAL)\n\nSnowflake `BIGINT`, `INT`, `INTEGER`, `SMALLINT`, `TINYINT`, `BYTEINT` are all **aliases for `NUMBER(38,0)`**. A Snowflake `BIGINT` column can hold values up to 10^38 — far beyond a 64-bit signed integer.\n\nDBSQL `BIGINT` is a true 64-bit signed integer (max 9,223,372,036,854,775,807 ≈ 2^63−1). Migrating Snowflake `BIGINT` to DBSQL `BIGINT` silently overflows for larger values.\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake\ncol BIGINT       -- actually NUMBER(38,0), holds up to ~1.0e38\n\n-- DBSQL (WRONG for large values — overflow at 2^63)\ncol BIGINT\n\n-- DBSQL (SAFE — preserves Snowflake range)\ncol DECIMAL(38,0)\n\n-- DBSQL (OK only when values are known to fit in 64-bit range)\ncol BIGINT\n```\n\nRule of thumb: if the source column has values sourced from external systems (user IDs, transaction IDs that may grow unboundedly), use `DECIMAL(38,0)`. If values come from a Snowflake `AUTOINCREMENT` sequence and will not exceed 2^63, `BIGINT` is fine.\n\n### FLOAT drift\n\nSnowflake `FLOAT`, `FLOAT4`, `FLOAT8`, `DOUBLE`, `REAL` all store as 64-bit IEEE doubles. DBSQL `FLOAT` is 32-bit, `DOUBLE` is 64-bit. Always migrate Snowflake float variants to DBSQL `DOUBLE` to preserve precision.\n\n### Decimal arithmetic precision\n\nDatabricks arithmetic on `DECIMAL` can produce different results due to precision promotion rules. Division increases precision; if the combined precision exceeds 38, rounding occurs.\n\n```sql\n-- Cast to smaller precision before division to avoid runaway precision\nSELECT CAST(a AS DECIMAL(18,4)) / CAST(b AS DECIMAL(18,4));\n```\n\nThis matches Snowflake's scale-promotion behavior in principle but produces differently-rounded intermediate results for chained arithmetic. Compare output on representative data if the pipeline depends on specific precision.\n\n### Conversion functions (scalar)\n\n| Snowflake                            | DBSQL                                                   | Notes                                                                         |\n| ------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `CAST(expr AS type)`                 | `CAST(expr AS type)`                                    | Same                                                                          |\n| `expr::type`                         | `expr::type`                                            | Both support                                                                  |\n| `TRY_CAST(expr AS type)`             | `TRY_CAST(expr AS type)`                                | Same                                                                          |\n| `TO_CHAR(date, 'fmt')`               | `DATE_FORMAT(date, 'fmt')`                              | **Format string syntax differs** — see `references/snowflake/09-functions.md` |\n| `TO_NUMBER(str)` / `TO_DECIMAL(str)` | `CAST(str AS DECIMAL(p,s))`                             | Use explicit precision                                                        |\n| `TO_DATE(str, 'fmt')`                | `TO_DATE(str, 'fmt')`                                   | Format string syntax differs                                                  |\n| `TO_TIMESTAMP(str, 'fmt')`           | `TO_TIMESTAMP(str, 'fmt')`                              | Format string syntax differs                                                  |\n| `TRY_TO_NUMBER(str)`                 | `TRY_CAST(str AS DECIMAL(p,s))`                         |                                                                               |\n| `TRY_TO_DATE(str)`                   | `TRY_CAST(str AS DATE)` or `TRY_TO_DATE(str)`           |                                                                               |\n| `TRY_TO_TIMESTAMP(str)`              | `TRY_CAST(str AS TIMESTAMP)` or `TRY_TO_TIMESTAMP(str)` |                                                                               |\n\nFor semi-structured conversions (`TO_VARIANT`, `PARSE_JSON`, `TO_JSON`, `TO_ARRAY`, `TO_OBJECT`), see `references/snowflake/03-semi-structured.md`.\n\n### BOOLEAN literals\n\n| Snowflake                                                               | DBSQL                                                                                                   |\n| ----------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `TRUE` / `FALSE`                                                        | `true` / `false` (case-insensitive)                                                                     |\n| `'true'` / `'false'` / `'yes'` / `'no'` / `'t'` / `'f'` / `'1'` / `'0'` | All cast to BOOLEAN in both dialects                                                                    |\n| `'on'` / `'off'` in BOOLEAN context                                     | **Snowflake casts these to BOOLEAN; DBSQL does NOT** — rewrite to `'true'`/`'false'` or use `CASE WHEN` |\n\nSnowflake and DBSQL agree on the common string truthy values (`'true'`/`'false'`, `'yes'`/`'no'`, `'t'`/`'f'`, `'1'`/`'0'`). The drift is on `'on'` / `'off'`: Snowflake accepts them, DBSQL does not. Flag and rewrite any `CAST('on' AS BOOLEAN)` / `CAST('off' AS BOOLEAN)`.\n"}}]);
